#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""imsg-react — add or remove one classic or exact-emoji tapback on one message.

    imsg-react --guid <GUID> love             dry run: say what would happen
    imsg-react --guid <GUID> love --yes       add ❤️ (nothing to do if it is there)
    imsg-react --guid <GUID> love --remove --yes

Tapbacks: love like dislike laugh emphasize question.
Custom emoji: --emoji-stdin reads bounded JSON {"emoji":"…","name":"…"}.
The optional name only searches; an exact Unicode AX identity must match
before pressing, and type 2006/3006 plus associated_message_emoji verifies it.

The old note said sending a tapback needs SIP off. That was true of code
injection; it is not true of Accessibility. Every rendered bubble in Messages'
transcript exposes the tapbacks as AX custom actions ("Heart", "Thumbs up", ...)
and performing one does what the tapback menu does — measured on macOS 26.6,
SIP on, under the Accessibility grant imsg-read already holds. Going through
Messages means the tapback syncs like one sent from the phone.

macOS 27 took those actions off the bubbles (measured 27.0.1: a bubble offers
"Tapback Details…", "Reply…", "Copy" and the like, none of the six). Performing
"Tapback Details…" opens the Tapback Picker on that bubble, any bubble, with the
six as buttons of the same names (measured 27.2). The picker names the message
it opened on, and nothing is pressed unless that is the target's text. chat.db
still decides success.

macOS 27.2 custom emoji use public native AX APIs to find the presentation
and its detached CharacterPalette. A result must expose the exact sequence
twice (glyph wrapper and enabled static-text child). That wrapper's AXPress
is ignored on 27.2, so one frame-stable, globally hit-tested click selects it.
The target, main window/title, presentation and glyph are revalidated first.
Live self-thread receipts verified add, replace, remove and a ZWJ sequence;
see docs/reactions.md for the limits and tests. Never retry after a gesture.

How one run goes:
  1. chat.db names the target: the message's text, its side, its minute, and
     how many identical bubbles (same text, side and minute) sit below it;
     Recently Deleted rows are not drawn, so they are not counted.
     This account's tapback on the message is read from chat.db too, so an
     add that is already there, or a remove that is not, exits 0 untouched.
     The AX action toggles; deciding from chat.db is what keeps it from
     undoing something.
  2. Messages opens the conversation (imsg-read's address URL) and the bubble
     is found among the rendered ones by that description, paging the
     transcript up at most MAX_PAGES_UP pages. Nothing is performed while
     searching.
  3. The action is performed once, by its exact name, and never in the pass
     that scrolled: AX elements are addressed by their place in the tree,
     and a scroll renumbers the rows, so the same path can then name the
     neighbour. A pass that finds the bubble brings it into view and notes
     its frame; the press comes on a later pass that finds it at that same
     frame, reading its action, and then its label and frame in one Apple
     event, just before ("unsettled" after MAX_SETTLES passes that saw it
     move, "moved" if the last read disagrees; nothing is performed either
     way). An identical bubble that slides onto that very frame in between
     is invisible to AX; the stray check below names it. A bubble that
     does not offer that name is reported ("not-offered", with what it does
     offer) and left alone: Apple is free to rename a localized action in a
     point release, and the neighbouring action is never a substitute.
  4. chat.db is the referee. Success means a new tapback row from this
     account, of the requested type, appeared on the message within ROW_WAIT
     seconds. The screen proves nothing. Paging for the bubble stops after
     RUN_LIMIT seconds.

No retry. A press with no row after ROW_WAIT → exit 75, "no-row"; a second
press could land after a late first row and toggle it back. A new row of this
account on ANOTHER message in the conversation ends the run on any outcome,
success or error: exit 75, "stray", with that message's GUID. The other person
can see it, so it is named, never left as "no-row" or behind another error.
An osascript that times out or fails mid-run may have pressed anyway, so
chat.db is asked the same way: a row on the target is success, one elsewhere
a stray, none the error.

Consent: over ssh, nothing touches System Events until TCC.db shows it is
already allowed; a prompt at an empty Mac becomes a denial (#36). Exit 77.

Refused before Messages is touched: groups (not tested yet), tapback rows,
messages without text, attachments, links, and rich-link / app cards
(their AX labels differ). Custom emoji use an exact bubble action or picker
identity, never a guessed index, image sticker, or typed message. If Messages
does not expose that identity, the operation fails visibly without pressing.

Enabled from Linux by tapbacks=on in bridge.conf (the shim refuses otherwise).
Message text never rides argv: it goes to osascript on stdin.

Output: one JSON line on stdout; a reason on stderr when it fails.
Exit: 0 done or nothing to do · 1 not-offered · 64 usage · 65 unsupported
target · 69 Messages or chat.db unavailable · 70 a bug in this tool (the
exception is named) · 75 unverified (not-rendered, ambiguous, unsettled,
moved, no-row, stray, unexpected row, timeout, osascript, busy) · 77
Accessibility or, over ssh, Automation → System Events not granted yet.
"""
